    "story": "His own bandage pulled when he leaned, and he leaned anyway. The man on the stretcher was not moving, and the stillness of it was worse than any shouting — worse than the wound in his own head, which at least had the decency to hurt in a way he understood.\nHe had not been told to come here. Nobody had sent for him, and nobody would have blamed him for staying where he was, with his own dressing and his own dark. But the hand was already out before the thought had finished forming.\nA wounded man does not reach for another wounded man because he has strength to spare. He reaches because he knows what it feels like to lie there and listen to the boots go past.",
