    "story": "She put her hand to his brow the way you test a kettle — not roughly, not tenderly either, but the way you test a thing that might burn you. The skin was hot under her palm, and the heat told her what his silence would not.\nHe did not open his eyes. He did not need to; the hand on his forehead was answer enough, and the steadiness of it said someone had come back. Somewhere in the tent, a basin clinked and a voice asked for water.\nShe kept her hand where it was a moment longer than the check required. Then she took it away, and the taking-away was its own small promise: I will be back.",
    "notes": "The print's weight is in the touch. Her hand is on his brow, and everything in the frame bends around that single point of contact — the nurse leaning in, the man lying still, the painterly light holding both of them the way a held breath holds a room. The gesture is not heroic by the old measure. It is the oldest work there is, done with a patience the camera happened to catch. A collector hangs this one for the quiet it keeps — a portrait of care that does not ask to be admired, only to be believed, and the believing is what makes it stay on the wall.",
