    "story": "They stood the way men stand when a camera is pointed at them and the war is pointed somewhere else — still, but not relaxed, each of them holding his rifle the way you hold a thing you have already agreed to carry.\nThe sand was cold through their boots, and the hedgehogs rose behind them like the teeth of a place that had already been fought over. None of them looked at the obstacles. They looked at the lens, and the lens looked back, and for the length of a shutter's blink the whole company was in one place at one time.\nAfterward, they would scatter — to foxholes, to trucks, to the next name on the next order. The photograph would stay where they could not: a company on a beach, and the storm coming in behind them.",
    "notes": "A single portrait can hold one man's resolve; this one holds a company's. Rifles and boots are arranged on a beach the way a painter arranges a composition, with a storm-dark sky doing the work of a backdrop and the hedgehogs doing the work of a warning. No one man carries this frame — that is the point — and the painterly light treats the group as a single body, one that has stood together and will scatter together. A collector hangs this one for the rarity of the thing: a full company caught in a single frame, on a beach that has already been fought over, and the print keeps that one held moment the way the sand keeps a footprint — briefly, and entirely.",
