    "story": "He pushed the goggles up because the dust had won the argument. The lenses were filmed over, and a man who cannot see the ground in front of him is a man who will trip on it — so the goggles went up, and the dust came in, and he decided the dust was the lesser of the two.\nThe case in his hand had been carried since first light, and the strap had worn a line into his palm that would still be there at nightfall. He did not set it down. A man sets a thing down when he means to stay, and he did not mean to stay.\nSomewhere ahead, the road was waiting to be walked. He had already walked a good part of it, and the rest of it was not going to walk itself.",
    "notes": "Look first at the goggles. They are supposed to be down, over his eyes, doing their job — and instead they sit pushed up on the helmet, filmed with dust, doing nothing at all. That single choice is what makes the portrait a portrait rather than a catalogue of kit: a man has decided, on his own, that seeing the ground matters more than the regulation. The painterly light honors the decision, holding the dust and the leather and the tired young face in the same unhurried regard. A collector hangs this one for that human flicker — the moment a soldier stops being a uniform and becomes a man who has made up his mind, and the print keeps the decision the way the helmet keeps the goggles: up, and in plain sight.",
