    "story": "He smiled because the mist had made the whole morning feel like a secret. The field was soft with it, and the road had gone pale, and for the first time in days the war was a thing you could not see — only hear, somewhere far off, like a radio left on in another room.\nThe strap across his chest had rubbed a line into his shoulder, and the canteen at his hip knocked against his leg with every step, and none of it mattered, because the smile was doing the work that the marching could not: it was keeping him a man, and not a machine for walking.\nHe would walk through the mist and out the other side of it, and the smile would go with him. That was the arrangement he had made with himself, and it was holding.",
    "notes": "A genuine smile is the rarest thing in this series, and this frame has one. It arrives unguarded, in a mist that has softened everything except the man's face, and the painterly light holds the two — the mist and the smile — in a balance that makes the portrait feel less like a document and more like a promise. The strap across his chest and the mud on his boots say where he has been; the smile says where he is going, and the going is forward. A collector hangs this one for that rarity — a portrait that proves optimism was not the absence of the war, but a man's answer to it, and the answer is still standing.",
