    "story": "He had learned, somewhere along the way, that a smile could do the work of an order. The men under him did not need to be told twice when the telling came with a look like that — half invitation, half expectation — and the coat and the cap did the rest.\nThe glasses had fogged in the cold, and he had wiped them on the inside of his cuff the way a man wipes a thing he has wiped a hundred times before. Behind him, the camp was already moving, and the moving was the morning's business.\nHe did not hurry. A man who has seen enough mornings to know which ones matter does not hurry, and this one mattered — because the men were watching, and the watching was the command.",
    "notes": "Command, in this frame, is a smile. It is not the smile of a man who has forgotten the war; it is the smile of a man who has decided, on this morning, that the war will not have his face. The peaked cap and the trench coat give him the shape of authority, and the glasses give him the shape of a man who reads his orders before he gives them — a combination the painterly light holds in a balance that makes the portrait feel less like a rank and more like a person. A collector hangs this one for that balance — a portrait that proves command, in these pictures, is not shouted but worn, and the wearing is what makes the print feel like a man worth following.",
