    "story": "He kept his face turned to the canvas, not because he was hiding it, but because the looking itself was the work. Somewhere beyond the flap, the ground he had crossed was still crossed, and the men he had led were still out there, and the knowing of it sat in his chest like a stone.\nThe uniform sat on him the way a uniform sits on a man who has worn it through more weather than he can remember. It was not a new coat. It was not a clean coat. It was the coat of a man who had stopped noticing coats.\nRight and wrong had left him a long time ago. What was left was the next order, and the men who would carry it out, and the weight of a thing that had grown too heavy to be held by any one pair of hands.",
