    "story": "She leaned on the truck the way you lean on a friend — not because you need the help, but because the help is there, and the leaning is the point. The metal was warm from the sun, and the sun was the first warm thing she had felt all week.\nThe cap on her head had slipped a little to one side, and she had not fixed it, because a woman who has spent the morning on her feet does not spend the afternoon fixing a cap. The smile came easy, the way a smile comes when nobody is grading it.\nSomewhere behind her, the work was waiting to be done again. She would do it. But for a minute, she was a woman leaning on a truck, and the truck was a truck, and the war was somewhere else.",
    "notes": "A uniform is not supposed to lean, and this one does. She is not standing at attention and she is not posing; she is leaning on a truck in the warm sun, her cap slipped a little to one side, her smile arriving the way a smile arrives when nobody is grading it. The painterly light treats the plain uniform and the plain moment with the same unhurried regard it gives any formal portrait, and that regard is what makes the print hold a wall: a woman who has stopped performing and started being, in sepia light, on an ordinary afternoon. A collector buys this one for the afternoon it keeps — a servicewoman between duties, and the between is where the war let her be herself.",
