    "story": "The hand came across before the word did. It was not a handshake and not quite a pat — it was a thing in between, the kind of gesture a man makes when the news is good enough to touch and too big to say out loud.\nHe had been standing there for a while, cap straight, collar straight, waiting to be told what came next. The tents behind him were already moving, and the flag above them was already snapping, and the next thing was already on its way.\nThe hand on his chest said it first: whatever it was, he had done it. He felt the weight of it settle, the way a coat settles on shoulders that have learned to carry one. And then he nodded, and the nodding was the answer.",
    "notes": "A hand reaches in from the right, and the portrait changes. It cuts across the frame the way a sentence cuts across a conversation, and the painterly light holds the interruption the way it holds the young man's face: gently, and without apology. The peaked cap is straight, the collar is straight, and the tents behind him are already moving, which is what makes the print linger: a man caught in the one second when the news has reached him but the words have not, and a hand on his chest is doing the telling. A collector hangs this one for that second — a portrait that proves recognition, in these pictures, does not need a ceremony — a hand reaching across is ceremony enough.",
