    "story": "He had been a paratrooper long enough to know that the patch on his arm was not a decoration. It was a promise — that he would go where the planes went, and land where the landing was, and the landing was wherever the war said it was.\nThe helmet on his head wore a net, and the net wore the scratches and the dust of every field it had been in. He did not polish it. A man does not polish a thing that has kept him alive.\nHe looked at the camera because the camera was there. The rifle on his shoulder had stopped feeling heavy a long time ago, and the stopping was the measure of it: not the miles, not the jumps, but the weight that had gone from heavy to nothing.",
    "notes": "The patch is small and the man is not. It is a red and blue square with two white letters on his sleeve, and the painterly light holds it the way it holds the netted helmet and the rifle — as a mark, and as a promise. The man's face does the rest: older, weathered, and looking straight at the camera the way a man looks at a thing he has already agreed to. A collector hangs this one for that agreement — a paratrooper standing in ordinary light, and the ordinary light is what makes the print feel true, because a man who has worn the same patch through more than he can say does not need a battle to prove it.",
