    "story": "The wall is cold through her blouse, and she means to keep it that way — the colder the stone, the less she has to think about the lane beyond it and the ears she is straining for. The rifle is heavier than it looked in the hands of the men who carried it before her, and the satchel at her hip keeps sliding forward, the way everything she owns seems to slide forward now.\n\nYesterday she was the woman who brought the bread and knew which neighbours to trust. Today the bread is in the satchel and the rifle is across her knees, and the lane has to stay empty until the others come back. She holds still the way the wall holds still, and waits for a sound that belongs to them.",
    "notes": "A crouch that tells the story before the title does — her back to the wall, the rifle across her knees, the satchel slipping forward at her hip. The beret sits at its familiar tilt and the pale blouse catches the light against the weathered stone, so the eye goes straight to the watchfulness of her face. It is a rare, quiet frame of the civilian at war, and the kind of portrait that makes a wall feel like a front line.",
