    "story": "She puts the lipstick on last, after the cap is straight and the tie is knotted and the white jacket sits the way it should. The uniform is the Navy's, down to the last button — the red at her mouth is the morning's own signature, put on for nobody's inspection but her own.\n\nBehind her the flag hangs where it hung yesterday, and the day's work waits in the same neat stacks — the papers that keep the ships fed and the men accounted for, the kind of work nobody photographs and everybody depends on. She will sign her name a hundred times before lunch, and every signature will be straight, because a tired sailor reads a signature the way he reads a chart.",
    "notes": "The whites carry this frame — cream jacket, black tie, the officer's cap with its eagle device, all of it set against the deep blue of the flag behind her. The composition is a study in contrast: pale uniform against dark field, the red of the lipstick a single note of warmth, and a gaze that holds the viewer without asking for anything. It is one of the set's strongest portraits of the women who kept the Navy running, and it hangs with a quiet, formal authority.",
