    "story": "They walk the way men walk when the road is long and the talking is what carries them — shoulder to shoulder, packs swaying in time, the rifle riding easy on the one man's shoulder because his hands are busy with the story he is telling.\n\nThe story is about home, like all the stories are, and it does not matter that the other man has heard it before — he has heard it three times since breakfast and he will hear it twice more before the column stops, because a story about home is not told for the news in it. The dust rises from their boots and settles on the bedrolls, and the path goes on in front of them, and neither of them looks back.",
    "notes": "The view from behind is what makes this one — two men, shoulder to shoulder on a pale path, the packs and the slung rifle drawn in warm, muted tones against a wash of old paper. The composition leaves the road ahead of them and nothing else, so the viewer's eye walks with them into the distance instead of stopping at a face. It is a quiet, companionable portrait of the march and the talk that carries it, and it brings a calm, steady presence to any wall.",
