    "story": "The glasses are cold against the bridge of his nose, and they make the world small enough to survive — one strip of ground at a time, one hedgerow, one gap in the smoke where a path might be. He holds his breath while he looks, the way you hold it when you are listening for something, and the rifle stays where his hand left it, because his eyes are doing the fighting now.\n\nBehind him the other man waits, and waiting is its own kind of work — he has learned to read the back of a neck and know whether the news is good. Somewhere out there the road goes on, or it doesn't, and the glasses will tell him which. He lowers them once, wipes the dust off the lenses with the edge of his sleeve, and raises them again, because trouble is not something you find by looking once.",
    "notes": "The binoculars are the frame's centre of gravity — raised to his eyes, they turn a simple portrait into a moment of searching, and the other man waiting out of focus behind him gives the scene its tension. The painted haze softens the background into smoke and dust, so the eye stays on the face and the glass, where the story lives. It is a sharp, searching portrait of the scout's work, and it hangs with a focus that quieter frames can't match.",
