    "story": "The stripes on his sleeve are still new enough that he touches them when nobody is looking — not to check that they are there, but to remind himself that they are his. The rifle rides across his chest the way a sergeant's rifle should, muzzle up and hands where they belong, and behind him the flag is a wash of stars and stripes, the country he is walking away from and toward in the same step.\n\nBehind him the men walk in his rhythm, because that is what a sergeant is — the man whose step the others match without thinking about it. Yesterday he was one of them, carrying the same pack and watching the same sergeant's back. Today the pack is the same and the back is his, and the stripes are the new part of the bargain.",
    "notes": "The flag wash does the heavy lifting in this one — the stars and stripes behind him turn a marching portrait into a statement, and the sergeant's stripes on his sleeve answer it from the front. The rifle carried at port arms gives the frame its clean diagonal, and the men moving behind him add depth without taking the eye off the leader. It is a proud, declarative portrait of the American sergeant, and it hangs like a poster in the best sense of the word.",
