    "story": "The letters on his helmet are big enough to read from across the yard, which is the point — a military policeman does not hide. He wears the letters the way a sheriff wears a star, and the men he watches know the letters before they know his name, the way you know a warning before you read it.\n\nThe glasses keep slipping down his nose, and he keeps pushing them back with the heel of his hand, because a man who cannot see the road cannot keep it. Yesterday a truck full of boys took a wrong turn and he sent them back with a wave. Today the rifle stands at his shoulder and the yard is quiet, and he is waiting for the next wrong turn, the way a man waits for rain — knowing it will come, and hoping it will not.",
    "notes": "The letters on the helmet are the draw in this one — MP, painted bold and white across the steel, they turn a quiet portrait into a statement about the order behind the lines. The round glasses and the half-smile give the face its character, and the rifle standing upright at his shoulder anchors the frame in the soldier's world. It is a distinctive, characterful portrait of the military policeman, and it stands out on any wall the moment a viewer reads those two letters.",
