    "story": "The ladle is the measure of the day — one scoop, leveled off with the back of a knife, the way a cook measures when he has a whole queue to feed and a pot that has to last. The man at the pot does not hurry. He has learned that a queue of hungry men will wait for a proper scoop, and that a short scoop is the fastest way to start an argument.\n\nThe bowls are coming in from all sides now, held out by men who have already put their rifles down and their helmets off, and the steam rises between them like a curtain. He serves the second man the way he served the first, and the third the way he served the second — a fair scoop is the cook's own kind of discipline. Behind him the camp goes on with its work, and the pot gets lower, and nobody says the word for what comes after the last bowl is filled.",
    "notes": "The ladle is the star of this one — mid-scoop, leveled and unhurried, it gives the frame its action and its honesty, with the cook's grin at the camera to soften it. The great steaming pot and the wooden crates set the scene without clutter, and the warm, muted tones keep the whole picture in the register of a remembered afternoon rather than a staged one. It is a warm, humane portrait of the army's unglamorous work, and the kind of frame that makes a wall feel like a place with a table in it.",
