    "story": "The cup is warm in both hands, and he holds it the way a man holds a small fire — not for the coffee, which is thin and bitter and mostly hot water, but for the heat, which is real and goes straight into his fingers and up his arms. The tent canvas behind him smells of damp and rope, and the ground under him is the same ground it has been all week.\n\nYesterday he was in the air, and the air was louder than anything. Today the loudest sound is the click of the cup against his teeth, and the men talking in low voices about the next jump, and the way the canvas moves when the wind finds it. He drinks the coffee slowly, because a cup finished too fast is a cup that leaves you with nothing to hold, and he is not ready to be done holding something warm.",
    "notes": "The cup in both hands is the heart of this one — a small, plain tin cup, held the way a man holds warmth, and it turns a sitting portrait into a moment of rest between the jumps. The tent canvas behind him gives the frame its texture, and the smile on the young face keeps the mood from going solemn. It is a gentle, human portrait of the paratrooper at ease, and the kind of frame that makes a wall feel like a place to sit a while.",
