    "story": "The stethoscope is cold when it first touches the skin, and she has learned to warm it in her hands before she uses it, because a cold chestpiece makes a man flinch and a flinch makes a heartbeat harder to find. The cap sits on her hair the way it has sat since the morning, and her head is bowed because the listening takes both ears and all of her attention.\n\nYesterday the ward was full and the work did not stop. Today the ward is quieter, and the quiet is the kind that makes a nurse listen harder — for the breath that catches, for the heart that stumbles, for the small sounds that tell her whether a man is getting better or only getting used to it. She keeps her head bowed over the work in her hands, and when the camera is done she will go back to the listening, because that is what the stethoscope is for.",
    "notes": "The bowed head is what gives this frame its tenderness — the white cap, the red cross emblem, the stethoscope looped around her neck, all of it held in a quiet, sepia register that makes the portrait feel like a memory. The striped dress and the white apron add a soft pattern against the plain background, so the eye stays on the face and the listening. It is a gentle, reverent portrait of the nurse at her work, and the kind of frame that brings a calm to any wall it hangs on.",
