    "story": "The wind comes off the sea and straight into their faces, and they do not mind it — it is the first wind all week that has not smelled of a camp, and it carries the salt and the gulls and the sound of the water instead. They stand shoulder to shoulder because that is how they were told to stand, and the sand gets into their boots, and nobody says anything about it, because a beach day is a beach day, and nobody wants to complain first.\n\nYesterday they were at their desks and their switchboards and their wards, and the sea was a rumor behind the dunes. Today it is right there, gray and wide and indifferent, and the six of them are in front of it in their service dress, posing for a photograph that will go home in six envelopes to six different kitchens. They smile because the camera is waiting, and they stand still because the sea is not, and the contrast is the joke they will all be telling by tonight.",
    "notes": "The line is what makes this frame — six women, shoulder to shoulder on the sand, the gray sea behind them and the wind doing what it wants with their uniforms and their caps. The composition is a study in togetherness: six faces in a row, six pairs of boots in the sand, and a horizon that goes on past the edge of the picture. It is a rare group portrait in a set of single sitters, and it brings a sense of company to any wall it hangs on.",
