    "story": "She tips her face to the sun because the tent has been in shadow all morning, and the warmth on her cheeks is the first good feeling of the day. The cap sits on her hair the way it has sat since the morning, and her arms are behind her back the way a woman holds them when she is waiting for the word to go — patient, and not in any hurry to be told.\n\nYesterday was paperwork and a queue and a sergeant who never smiled. Today the sun is out and the camera is out, and the man behind it is asking her to look up, and she does, because looking up is the closest she has come to a holiday since the spring. She will go back to the tent when the photograph is done, and the shadow will take her face back, and the warmth will stay where it was — on her cheeks, where the sun put it, and nowhere else a sergeant can take it.",
    "notes": "The upturned face is what carries this one — the garrison cap at its tilt, the soft light on her cheeks, the smile that is half for the camera and half for the sun. The warm, sepia tone keeps the portrait in a gentle register, and the plain background leaves nothing to compete with the face. It is a sweet, simple portrait of the young servicewoman, and the kind of frame that brings a quiet light to any wall it hangs on.",
