    "story": "The laughter starts with one of them, and the others catch it the way you catch a cold — fast, and without asking — and the pile is the result, five women in uniform in a heap on the sand, arms around each other and caps askew, the sea behind them doing nothing at all about the noise.\n\nYesterday they stood in a line and did what they were told. Today the line is gone and the orders are gone, and there is nothing left to do but be in the photograph, and the photograph will show them exactly as they are — five women who have forgotten, for a minute, that the war is watching. The man behind the camera does not tell them to straighten up, because a pile is a better portrait than a line, and the laughter is what the people at home will want to see.",
    "notes": "The laughter is what makes this frame — five women in a heap on the sand, arms around each other, caps askew, the sea behind them in soft light, and the joy of it caught mid-burst. The composition is a tangle on purpose: no line, no order, just five friends who have forgotten the camera is there. It is the set's most joyful portrait of the women of the war, and the kind of frame that makes a wall feel like a good day.",
