    "story": "The cuff tightens around his arm the way it always tightens — a slow squeeze, and the gauge's needle climbs, and she watches the needle the way a woman watches a clock, counting down the seconds until the number is true. The stethoscope is in her ears and her fingers are on his wrist, and the two of them together tell her what his body is saying, and his body is saying it quietly, the way a tired man says everything.\n\nYesterday the cuff was on another man, and the number was a different number, and the tent smelled the same — of canvas and antiseptic and the dust that gets into everything. Today the needle settles and she writes the number down in the book she keeps at her side, because a number in a book is a man who was looked after, and a man who was looked after is a man who has a chance. She lets the cuff go, and his arm comes back to him, and the gauge rests, and the tent goes on with its work.",
    "notes": "The gauge is what holds this frame — the round dial in her hand, the needle inside it, the cuff around his arm, all of it held in a quiet, sepia register that makes the portrait feel like a moment of real work. The nurse's bowed head and the soldier's stillness give the composition its tenderness, and the stethoscope looped at her neck reminds the viewer what the listening is for. It is a careful, watchful portrait of the field hospital at work, and it brings a quiet, steady presence to any wall it hangs on.",
