    "story": "The bell on the cart is the sound of summer, and the sailor has been hearing it since the street turned the corner — a small, bright bell that means ice cream, and ice cream means a day off, and a day off means the war can wait an hour. The vendor is in his whites and his cap, and his hands are already in the cart, and the woman in the red dress is deciding between the flavors the way a woman decides between two good things, slowly, because the deciding is part of the treat.\n\nYesterday the sailor was on a ship, and the ship was cold in a way that had nothing to do with ice cream. Today the cold is in a cone, and the cone is in his hand, and the bicycle is leaning against the cart the way a bicycle leans when its owner has found something better to do. He will pedal home when the ice cream is gone, and the woman will walk beside him, and the vendor will ring the bell for the next street, and the summer will go on being summer, which is the nearest this street has come to peace in a long time.",
    "notes": "The cart is the star of this one — white, with its red wheels and its painted sign, it anchors a whole street scene in a single frame, and the three figures around it give the portrait its story: the woman in the rose dress, the sailor with his bicycle, the vendor in his whites. The sepia light keeps the scene warm and unhurried, and the composition leaves room for the grass and the trees, so the viewer can hear the cart's bell in the quiet. It is a rare, gentle portrait of the home front at its sweetest, and it brings a summery calm to any wall it hangs on.",
