    "story": "His mouth is open because the call has to be made, and it falls to him to make it — a name, or a warning, or the word that means move, and the saying of it has to carry through the trees the way a bell carries through a village. The netted helmet sits heavy on his head, and the scarf is at his throat, and the rifles of the men behind him point at nothing yet, which is exactly how he wants it to stay.\n\nYesterday the trees were quiet and the birds had the place to themselves. Today his voice has the place, and the birds are gone, and the men behind him wait for the echo to come back and tell them whether they are alone here or not. He will call again if he has to, and the call will go out the same way, and the trees will give it back the same way, until the answer comes or the silence does.",
    "notes": "The open mouth is what gives this frame its life — the call caught mid-word, the netted helmet heavy on his head, the rifles of the men behind him rising like punctuation into the trees. The composition keeps the young face large and the background soft, so the viewer hears the call even where there is no sound. It is a vivid, urgent portrait of the infantryman in the moment of the shout, and it brings a voice to any wall it hangs on.",
