    "story": "The life vest is heavy on his shoulders, and it smells of canvas and salt and the sweat of the men who wore it before him, and he leans on the side of the craft because the craft is solid and the water is not, and the difference between the two is why his arm is on the rail and not in the sea.\n\nYesterday the craft was a training ground and the sea was a view. Today the craft is the way in and the sea is the way to it, and the beach is somewhere beyond the gray, and the men behind him are quieter than they have been all week, because quiet is what men do when the talking is done and the doing is about to start. He keeps his glasses on his nose and his hand on the rail, and he watches the water the way a man watches a road he has to cross, and the mess is coming, and he knows it, and the knowing is the job.",
    "notes": "The life vest is what sets this frame apart — gray and bulky, tied in front, it turns a portrait into a moment just before the landing, and the older face above it gives the frame its gravity. The landing craft's rail at his elbow and the sea behind him keep the composition simple, so the eye goes to the glasses and the steady gaze, and the wait in them. It is a tense, honest portrait of the man about to go in, and it brings a sense of the beach to any wall it hangs on.",
