    "story": "The rope is in his hand because a man on a craft holds on to something, and the rope is the something that is there — thick and salt-stiff, smelling of the sea and the tar that keeps it from rotting, and he holds it the way you hold a thing you know you will have to let go of soon.\n\nThe man behind him is waiting the same way, and the sea is doing the same thing it has done all morning, which is nothing at all, and the nothing is the worst of it — a quiet sea is a sea that has not decided yet, and a craft full of men is a place where everybody has already decided and is only waiting to be told. The ramp is in front of him and the beach is beyond it, and when the ramp goes down the rope will not matter any more, and neither will the waiting, and that is the part he is counting on.",
    "notes": "The rope is the detail that makes this frame — thick and salt-stiff in his hand, it gives the portrait its grip, and the life vest and the landing craft give it its place. The older face above them, the glasses and the steady gaze, turn a waiting portrait into a portrait of a man who has already decided, and the sea behind him in the gray light keeps the scene in the register of the morning before the landing. It is a tense, honest portrait of the naval man on the morning of the landing, and it holds a wall with the steady patience of the man it shows.",
