    "story": "The panel on his chest is the reason he cannot bend, and the reason he cannot bend is why his mouth is doing the work — the shout has to come out of his mouth because his hands are busy with the black thing at his waist, and his shoulders are busy with the straps, and there is nothing left to move but his voice.\n\nYesterday the panel was in a bag and the bag was on a truck. Today the panel is on him and the truck is gone, and the men behind him are waiting for the word that means the work can start. He gives it to them the way a man gives an order in a field — loud, and once, and from the chest, because the chest is where the voice lives when the hands are full. He will give it again if he has to, and the panel will stay where it is, and the straps will hold, and the work will start when the word does.",
    "notes": "The chest panel is what gives this frame its weight — broad and gray, strapped across his chest, it turns a portrait into a study of the gear a man carries, and the open mouth above it gives the frame its voice. The helmet sits at its familiar tilt and the soft green backdrop keeps the mood from going stern, so the eye stays on the face and the call. It is a strong, characterful portrait of the soldier in his gear, and it brings a sense of the working war to any wall it hangs on.",
