    "story": "The paper has been folded and unfolded so many times the creases are soft as cloth, and he still holds it like it might tear. The words are the same ones he read on the march, and they land differently here, standing still in the dust with his canteen cup swinging against his hip. Somebody at home wrote them at a kitchen table, and for a minute that table is closer than the hill behind him. Then he folds it again, tucks it where it rides safe, and looks back toward the road, because the day is not done waiting on him.",
    "notes": "Collectors will point to the letter first, because it is what makes the portrait stop you: a folded page held with more care than the rest of his kit, set against a helmet and uniform rendered in the soft, painterly light that defines Wayne Heim's Facing History work. The profile composition — chin up, eyes past the frame, the metal cup still in his other hand — gives the piece a stillness that reads from across a room, and the warm hillside palette makes it an easy anchor in a WWII collection. It is the kind of image that starts a conversation about the mail that kept men going.",
