    "story": "His hand stays up until they are past, because that is the rule and because somebody out there earned it. The column keeps step behind him, boots finding the same beaten earth, helmets level, eyes forward — nobody looks at the camera, nobody breaks the line. He is not thinking about the salute; he is thinking about the road after the review, and whether the truck that is supposed to be there will be there. The salute ends when the last man is by, and then they are only a column again, walking toward whatever comes next.",
    "notes": "Buyers notice the salute first, and then they notice that nobody else in the column is looking at it — every face stays forward, every boot stays in step, which is what makes the frame feel like a real moment rather than a posed one. Wayne Heim's painterly treatment warms the khaki and the dust into a single sepia register, so the piece hangs like a print from the period itself while reading clearly as fine art. It is a strong anchor for any WWII collection.",
