    "story": "The grin lands first, and it does not belong to the day — the cart has stopped, the road is dust, and the men around him are looking anywhere but at the camera. He grins anyway, because somebody has to, because the alternative is standing there thinking about how far there is still to go. His hands rest on the cart's edge the way a man's hands rest on a fence at home, and for a moment the war sits behind him instead of in front of him.",
    "notes": "There is a rare, unguarded warmth in this frame that collectors will return to: the broad grin of the man in the field cap, set against the weary, elsewhere faces of his comrades, all of it held in Wayne Heim's soft painterly light. The wooden cart edge anchoring the foreground gives the composition a grounded, working-life weight, and the contrast between one man's laughter and the others' silence is what keeps the portrait talking. Hung large, it carries a room on the strength of that one grin.",
