    "story": "The canvas seat is not made for comfort and he has stopped expecting it to be. The engines are still quiet, the curtain by the door still hangs straight, and the light coming through is the kind that belongs to late afternoon fields, not to the inside of an airplane. He sits with his hands loose and his legs crossed like a man in a waiting room, because that is what this is — a waiting room that happens to fly. Whatever is on the other side of that door will want him ready, so he is ready, and in the meantime he lets the quiet do its work.",
    "notes": "The stillness is what sells this one: an older serviceman at complete rest inside a machine built for urgency, his hands loose, his gaze level, the cabin softened by Wayne Heim's painterly light into something closer to a parlor than a fuselage. The flag patch on his sleeve and the cargo netting around him root the frame firmly in the war's transport world, and the composition — a man, a seat, a stretch of waiting — is the kind of understatement that rewards the room it hangs in. Quiet frames like this are the ones visitors stand in front of longest.",
