    "story": "She reads the words the way you read them when they have to land right — slowly, one finger holding the place, her lips shaping each one before it leaves her mouth. The book is heavier than it looks, and the list inside it is longer than anyone wants it to be, but she does not rush, because the names on the page deserve to be said right. The tents are quiet behind her back. The metal churn on the table is cold and sweating in the morning air. When she finishes, the day will start moving again, and it will move the way she tells it to.",
    "notes": "A collector buys this frame for the concentration in it: a woman reading aloud as if the words were orders themselves, her finger holding the place, the dark uniform and peaked cap drawn with a clarity that lets every detail of the camp — the tents, the churn, the grass — sit in soft painterly focus around her. It is a portrait of authority without a single raised voice, and it hangs with a gravity that suits a study or a gallery wall. The book never quite gives up what it says, and that is what keeps a room coming back to it.",
