    "story": "The churn is still cold from the morning, and her hand has been resting on it so long the metal has started to take the warmth of her palm. Everything else is moving — the tents breathe a little in the breeze, the grass bends, and somewhere behind her somebody is doing the same work she is about to go back to. For now the camera has her, and she gives it the smile she keeps for the moments when the work pauses. Then the churn will be heavy again, and she will carry it anyway.",
    "notes": "The smile is what a buyer takes home from this one: a young woman in a belted work jumpsuit, caught between tasks in a tented camp with her hand on a milk churn, giving the camera the easy, unperformed look that staged portraits almost never achieve. Wayne Heim's painterly treatment warms the olive jumpsuit and the canvas tents into one soft register, and the churn gives the composition a working anchor that keeps it from floating into pretty. Hung in a run of camp portraits, it is the warm one — the frame visitors stop at.",
