    "story": "The sound reaches them before anything does — a low drone that turns both their heads up at the same instant, one hand shading her eyes, the other arm already pointing. They know that note by now, the way you know a voice in a crowded room, and for two seconds the churn and the tents and the morning fall away behind the sky. Then the sound moves past, the way it always does, and their hands come down, and the work is still there waiting, the same as it was before the sky interrupted.",
    "notes": "Two women looking up is a rarer composition than one, and this frame earns its place on a wall by the pairing alone: one arm raised in a point, one hand shading her eyes, both of them caught in the same half-second of sky-watching. Wayne Heim's painterly light warms the olive jumpsuits and the canvas tents into a single soft register, and the milk churn between them anchors the pair in the working camp where the frame was made. It is the most alive of the camp portraits — the one where the moment does the talking.",
