    "notes": "The lean is what makes this frame: a servicewoman resting her hand on the white star painted on her jeep, smiling at the camera with the ease of someone who has stopped posing and started owning the moment. Wayne Heim's painterly treatment gives the image the depth of an aged photograph — the sepia tones, the soft border — so it reads as a keepsake from the war itself rather than a picture of one. The jeep, the star, the tents behind her: everything in the frame says she belongs to a working world, and the smile is what a collector will remember. It hangs best where the light lets the sepia do its work.",
