    "story": "The stripes are new enough that he still feels them on his arm when he moves — three bars of cloth that changed the way every man in the room looks at him, and the way he has to look at himself. The tie is knotted the way the day demands, the shirt is pressed, and the light in the room is warm enough to make the whole sitting feel like a parlor instead of a photograph. He is not looking at the camera. He is looking past it, at the work that will not wait for a portrait, and at the men who will be standing behind him when it comes. A sergeant is not a rank you wear. It is a weight you carry, and he is still learning how it sits.",
    "notes": "The chevron is what a collector will go straight to: three stripes on a young sergeant's sleeve, set against a dark painterly backdrop that lets the rank and the gaze carry the frame. Wayne Heim's treatment warms the olive uniform and the skin into a single soft register, and the profile composition — the face turned away from the camera, the eyes on something past it — gives the portrait a gravity that front-facing frames rarely achieve. It is a portrait of rank worn young, and of the weight that comes with it, and it holds a wall the way a good sergeant holds a room.",
